        "details": {
            "blurb": "",
            "languages": {
                "details": "Truespeech",
                "value": [
                    "common",
                    "fey"
                ]
            },
            "level": {
                "value": 14
            },
            "privateNotes": "",
            "publicNotes": "<p>Consider a lord on a festival day, fair-faced and merry in his cups, extending his largess to any who please him. Consider that lord on a harvest day, a tyrant in the fields, demanding work he would not do himself. Consider that lord on his commendation day, swearing to protect and nurture the lands and its people in exchange for fealty and troth from those very same subjects.</p>\n<p>Consider a green knight, great and green of skin and green of armor, who might appear on such a day to that lord.</p>\n<p>The green knights are avatars of honor and of one's word given and kept. They're duty-bound to ensure others, especially those of high station, hold true to the promises they make. Sallying forth from their keeps in the depths of the First World, the green knights arrive anywhere nobility falters: where a lord forgets his vassal vows, a knight wavers in her cause, or an oath maker finds themself on the path of betrayal, a green knight arrives to test their worth. They serve as self-appointed judge, jury, and sometimes executioner, offering boons to those who pass their tests and banes for those who fail.</p>\n<p>The greatest among the green knights answer to Magdh herself, who sends them forth to judge and guide mortals who find themselves at such crossroads. Some answer to different Eldest or to older and stranger powers, and others follow only their own codes. Even those green knights who swear no fealty to a higher power, however, have the uncanny ability to find those most deserving of their judgment.</p>\n<p>Green knights tower over those they challenge, standing 8 feet tall or more and wearing thick green armor that makes them seem larger still. Despite their intimidating presence, they're genteel and graceful, with perfectly coiffed hair, elegantly trimmed beard, and flawless etiquette. They invariably carry massive bronze axes, symbols of their station, but—as courtly graces demand—they rarely brandish their weapons unless faced with harm. However, should someone be foolhardy enough to challenge a green knight to martial combat, they might find their head quickly and cleanly severed from their body with one mighty blow from that axe.</p>",
            "publication": {
                "license": "ORC",
                "remaster": true,
                "title": "Pathfinder Feybound"
            }
        },
        "initiative": {
